WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.467
Preview: the order changed. The kitchen has the earlier version.

2
00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:14.400
Built by Myke Mueller, Never86’d founder and Community Tap & Pizza operator.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:23.900
Start with the exact synthetic message Muse reviewed.

4
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:32.133
Muse checked dated menu facts and the 12-versus-13 count.

5
00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:40.633
Adopt the model draft. Its source stays attached; the conflict stays open.

6
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:51.933
An authored guest clarification makes it 13 pizzas. The quote is $213.

7
00:00:51.933 --> 00:01:03.100
The operator reviews price, timing and capacity for this exact version.

8
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:12.433
Two simulated receipts acknowledge version 3: six pizzas, then seven.

9
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:22.833
One more garden pizza creates version 4. The old approval cannot cover it.

10
00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:34.167
The operator reviews “pickup only” for this restaurant and this date.

11
00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:41.233
The reviewed rule is saved locally. Demo orders still reset on restart.

12
00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:50.267
After a real server restart: the correction returns; demo orders reset.

13
00:01:50.267 --> 00:02:00.167
Three local checks confirm the rule applies only to this restaurant today.

14
00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:12.433
A separate recorded Muse review retrieves the restored rule and preserves delivery.

15
00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:24.133
The courier claim exceeds the source. The operator edits an unsent reply.

16
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:33.400
Adopt the five-pizza draft. Delivery stays requested, and quoting stays blocked.

17
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:43.000
Only explicit, authored guest consent changes the request to pickup.

18
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:49.967
Action Shift: keep the source, the owner’s decision and the right handoff together.
